[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mSection 4 — Testing and tooling[0m

  [38;5;153mcore.js[39m being pure is what makes this testable: level filtering, redaction (including nested, circular, and [38;5;153mError[39m
  values), and ring-buffer eviction are all exercisable by calling [38;5;153mcreateLogger[39m with a fake sink and a fixed [38;5;153mnow[39m — no
  DOM, no process, no timing. The browser adapter's rehydrate-and-persist cycle is testable the same way by injecting a
  fake [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m object, which is worth doing because "logs survive a reload" is a claim that's easy to believe and
  easy to get wrong.

  This repo has no test runner, no linter, and no dependencies at all, so I'd reach for Node's built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[39m and
  [38;5;153mnode:assert[39m — real tests, [38;5;153mnode --test[39m, still zero dependencies. That's my default unless you'd rather pull in a
  framework.

  The repo also has no tooling configured generally, and setting it up is cheapest right now, before the code exists.
  Worth deciding once here so it lands in the spec and every later task inherits it.
